---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="[Company A]: 3x Pipeline from Google Ads"
  description="How I rebuilt a fragmented paid search program to generate $[X]M in qualified pipeline within 6 months — without increasing budget."
  tag="Paid Search"
  company="[Company A] — anonymized"
  industry="B2B SaaS / [Vertical]"
  duration="[X] months"
  datePublished="2024-01-01"
  results={[
    { label: "Pipeline generated", value: "$[X]M" },
    { label: "Pipeline increase", value: "3x" },
    { label: "Cost per lead reduction", value: "[X]%" },
  ]}
>

  <h2>The situation</h2>
  <p>
    [Describe the company's state when you arrived or took on the program. What was broken,
    underperforming, or missing? What were the business pressures — funding round, slowing growth,
    new leadership expectations? Provide enough context that the reader understands the stakes.]
  </p>

  <h2>The challenge</h2>
  <p>
    [What made this hard? Technical complexity, organizational friction, data gaps, a fragmented
    account structure, poor attribution, a depleted ICP? Be specific — vague problems don't make
    credible case studies.]
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>[Specific challenge 1]</li>
    <li>[Specific challenge 2]</li>
    <li>[Specific challenge 3]</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The approach</h2>
  <p>
    [Walk through what you actually did — structured as decisions, not a task list. What did you
    diagnose first? What did you change and why? What was the reasoning behind key calls?]
  </p>
  <p>
    [This is where your expertise shows. Don't just list tactics — explain the thinking behind them.
    "We restructured campaigns by intent stage because the original setup was mixing branded and
    non-branded terms, which made optimization signals unreliable."]
  </p>

  <h2>The results</h2>
  <p>
    [State the outcomes clearly and tie them to business impact. Pipeline, CAC, CPL, ROAS,
    influenced revenue, sales cycle velocity — whatever metrics matter most in this context.
    If you can show before/after, do it.]
  </p>

  <h2>What I learned</h2>
  <p>
    [One or two honest takeaways — what surprised you, what you'd do differently, what this
    case revealed about B2B paid search more broadly. This section is optional but makes the
    case study feel like a real reflection, not a sales pitch.]
  </p>

</CaseStudyLayout>
